--- stdout
{"command": "prime", "ok": true, "data": {"guide": "# OKF Knowledge Catalogue — agent onboarding\n\nThe catalogue is a read-mostly, LLM-facing knowledge store, separate from any\nObsidian vault it may be opened in. It holds thematic *bundles*; a bundle is a\nfolder of Markdown *concepts*, each with YAML frontmatter carrying a non-empty\n`type`. A concept-id is its path below the bundle, minus `.md`. The store is a\nplain git repo — ordinary file tools and git apply.\n\nEvery invocation needs `--root <catalogue>`; without it the CLI searches cwd and\nits ancestors for a catalogue marker and fails if it finds none. It never falls\nback to its own script directory.\n\n## Retrieve — by command, not by blind grep\n\nProgressive disclosure: widen only when the cheaper step left the question open.\n\n  1. find \"<query>\"             ranked search across all bundles; narrow it with\n                                --where \"type = Runbook and path ~ <bundle>/\"\n  2. index [<bundle>]           what bundles and directories exist\n  3. toc <bundle>[/<concept>]   headings before bodies — the cheapest way to\n                                decide whether a page is worth reading. A bare\n                                <bundle> or <bundle>/<subdir> resolves to its\n                                index.md, so the names listed below are the\n                                argument, verbatim.\n  4. read <bundle>/<concept>    the page itself. `read --section \"<heading>\"`\n                                prints one section; `read --follow N` adds the\n                                pages it links to (`--inbound`: also those\n                                linking to it)\n  5. refs / wikilinks           follow the graph out of a page\n\nReach for `toc` before `read`: a concept can run long, and its headings usually\nsettle the question or name the section to read. Concepts cross-link in\nMarkdown — following a link beats re-running a search, and a large bundle's\n`index.md` is the map its concepts hang from.\n\n## Write — through the ce-okf skill, never by hand\n\nConcepts are not hand-authored. The `ce-okf` skill routes a source to a bundle,\napplies frontmatter and re-indexes. `index.md` listings are marker-managed and\ngenerated (`reindex`) — never edit between the `<!-- okf:... -->` markers. Tag\nedits go through `merge-tags` / `remove-tags`, not a search-and-replace. A\ncommit is allowed only once `check` reports zero violations.\n", "commands": "commands by purpose:\n  navigation:\n    ctx              context bundles: curated, ordered reading lists per topic (`ctx` lists\n                     them, `ctx <slug>` shows one)\n    index            catalogue TOC (bundle names + one-line descriptions), or one bundle's\n                     index.md\n    info             print catalogue schema docs (TYPES.md + PROPERTIES.md)\n    list             list the complete, unranked set of concepts a required --where expression\n                     selects\n    prime            one-shot agent onboarding: usage guide, command directory, catalogue state\n                     and bundle TOC\n    read             print one concept page\n    recent           list the most recently created concepts, newest first\n    sync-export      print a canonical block's payload for materialisation elsewhere\n    toc              print a concept's headings (default: h2 only)\n    visualize        write a viz.html concept graph for a bundle (embeds every concept body;\n                     needs network for cytoscape/marked)\n  search:\n    build-index      (re)build the FTS5 lexical search index at <root>/.fts/fts.sqlite (local\n                     only, no network)\n    build-semantic-index fill <root>/.tagvec/tag_vectors.sqlite's concept_emb table for every\n                     concept still missing a voyage-3-large embedding (NETWORK, requires\n                     VOYAGE_API_KEY)\n    find             hybrid ranked search: lexical FTS5/BM25 arm plus semantic arm\n    refs             list inbound and outbound markdown links of a file\n  tags:\n    merge-tags       merge/rename one or more tags into a target tag across concept frontmatter\n    normalize-tags   convert inline `tags: [...]` fields to YAML block-list form (local\n                     convention)\n    remove-tags      remove one or more tags from concept frontmatter (no merge target)\n    tags             list all tags with usage counts, or fuzzy-search candidate tags\n  maintenance:\n    archive          move a concept into _archive/<bundle>/, or back with --restore (dry-run\n                     default)\n    check            conformance scan: §9 hard rules + local invariants\n    fix-crosslinks   rewrite links to catalogue-root-absolute form (/bundle/subdir/concept.md)\n    fix-xml-tags     wrap `<tag>`/`<tag>.<tag2>` placeholders in inline code\n    install          register okf, its agent skill, MCP server and shell completion with this\n                     machine's agents and shells (dry-run default; needs no catalogue)\n    migrate          migrate frontmatter to OKF 0.2: `timestamp` to `generated` (git), `status`\n                     per D37 (dry-run default)\n    registry         reformat registry entries of TAGS.md or TYPES.md (table or block form);\n                     prose stays\n    reindex          (re)generate marker-bounded index listings: index.md one level, index-\n                     map.md the whole subtree\n    rename           move a concept + rewrite every inbound/outbound link + reindex (dry-run\n                     default)\n    rewrite-path     literal path-string rewrite across text files\n    sync-insert      materialise a canonical block inside a consumer concept (dry-run default)\n    sync-status      drift report for every materialised canon block\n    sync-update      re-materialise outdated consumer blocks from their canon owner (dry-run\n                     default)\n    verify           record that a concept was checked against its sources\n    wikilinks        report Obsidian wikilinks ([[…]]) and whether each resolves to a concept", "find": "## find — the entry point, in full\n\nusage: okf find [-h] [--include-topics] [--limit LIMIT] [--where EXPR] [--breakdown]\n                [--expand-tags] [--json]\n                query\n\nhybrid ranked search: lexical FTS5/BM25 arm plus semantic arm\n\npositional arguments:\n  query             search terms; quote multi-word queries\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help        show this help message and exit\n  --include-topics  widen beyond .okf.toml declared bundle to topic folders\n  --limit LIMIT     max results to print (default 10)\n  --where EXPR      select concepts by an expression: `field op value` with op one of = != < <= >\n                    >= ~ (contains), or `field in (a, b)`; combine with and, or, not and\n                    parentheses. Fields: type, tag, path (<bundle>/<concept-id>), archived (true\n                    or false), tier (unverified, machine-confirmed or human-reviewed), stale (true\n                    or false), status (an absent status is stable) and any frontmatter key\n                    (nested: a.b; through lists: verified.by, sources.resource). Timestamp keys\n                    (generated.at, verified.at, stale_after, usage_window.from/to,\n                    sources.last_modified, sources.usage_window.from/to) compare as instants and\n                    take an ISO 8601 value with an explicit offset: stale_after <\n                    2026-10-01T00:00:00Z. Archived concepts are hidden unless the expression names\n                    `archived`. Quote a value holding spaces, + or other characters: 'a b'.\n                    Example: (type = Runbook and tag != draft) and path ~ home-lab/\n  --breakdown       print per-hit contributing field(s)/term(s); output is byte-identical to today\n                    without this flag\n  --expand-tags     expand the query with semantically near registered tags before lexical\n                    scoring; measured against the full catalogue and found ineffective for the\n                    vocabulary-mismatch class it was built for, so it stays off by default\n                    (Q05/D17)\n  --json            print one JSON envelope {command, ok, data, warnings, error}\n\nrank / score lines:\n  Every hit's headline number is `rank: N` — its 1-based position in the\n  delivered list. It is the one scale comparable across a mixed-arm result,\n  because it IS the list order, and it claims nothing about the two arms'\n  raw scores being on one scale (D12: fuse_conditional hands the ranking to\n  whichever arm owns it, it never blends the two by score). Those raw,\n  non-comparable values stay out of the default output and show only with\n  --breakdown, one `score:` line per hit naming which arm produced it:\n    a/100        the FTS5/BM25 lexical arm, relative to this run's own top\n                 hit.\n    a/(6 * n)    the substring fallback used when no FTS index is available:\n                 per query term 3 for a title hit, 2 for a tag hit, 1 for a\n                 body hit, capped at one per field per term — so the maximum\n                 is 6 * number of terms.\n    cosine X.XX  the semantic arm's raw cosine similarity — printed on its\n                 own line, not folded into an a/100 fraction, because that\n                 shape would invite comparing it against a lexical score\n                 (D12, same rule as above).\n  `provenance:` names the arm that produced the line. Degradations (index\n  absent or stale, embedding backend unavailable) are announced on stderr,\n  one note per degraded arm.\n\nfilters:\n  --where EXPR selects the concepts the answer may hold, whichever arm owns\n  the ranking: the lexical, substring and semantic arms all apply it before\n  the result is cut to --limit. Without `archived` in the expression,\n  archived concepts are hidden; the semantic arm never covers them.", "state": "## This catalogue\n\nroot: <ROOT>\nbundles: 4 · concepts: 24\nfts index: absent — `find` degrades to a substring scan; run `build-index`\nsemantic index: absent — `find` runs its lexical arm only\ntag vocabulary: closed — 11 tags in TAGS.md; check a new one with `tags --validate`, adopt it with `tags --register`\nctx bundles: 3 — cooking · legacy · onboarding — `ctx show <slug>` lists one\nroot docs: SPEC.md · TYPES.md · PROPERTIES.md · TAGS.md", "bundles": ["bundle: _ctx\ndescription: Curated, ordered reading lists per topic — one file per ctx slug, managed by `okf ctx`.\nconcepts: 3", "bundle: handbook\ndescription: Welcome to the handbook.\nconcepts: 10\ndirectories:\n- ops", "bundle: kc-gov\ndescription: \nconcepts: 2", "bundle: recipes\ndescription: \nconcepts: 9\ndirectories:\n- drafts\n- seasonal"]}, "warnings": [], "error": null}
